Commit the clean-up in small batches with an optional pause

With the patches numbered before this one, `barbican-manage db clean`
commits 10,000 rows per transaction (`DEFAULT_CLEANUP_BATCH_SIZE` in
barbican/model/clean.py) and has no option to change that; only the
legacy `barbican-db-manage clean` takes `--batch-size`.

On a Galera cluster one transaction is one write set. The other
members apply the write sets with their applier thread, one at a time
and in commit order, so a 10,000-row write set holds up every later
commit until they have applied it. While they do, their receive queues
pass the flow control limit and the cluster pauses the commits of
every database. The MariaDB of a ControlPlane runs one applier thread
(mariadb-operator's default for `replicaThreads`). In the
C5C3/playground cluster one clean-up run paused the cluster for 20 to
65 % of every minute, and during the eight runs of 2026-11-18 the
public gateway answered 73 requests with 5xx, among them Keystone 503
after a commit waited past the 10 s read timeout of the API containers
(C5C3/cobaltcore#1830).

Measured on 2026-11-19 on three mariadb:12.3 (12.3.3) containers as
one Galera cluster with gcs.fc_limit=16 and a 24 MB buffer pool, so
that the deletes are bound by disk reads. Each run cleans 240,000 rows
(30,000 soft-deleted secrets with their metadata) through member 1,
while eight connections commit single-row inserts into another
database on member 1 at 40 commits/s in total:

  rows   pause  appliers      run  longest    over 1 s  paused
  10,000     0         1    572 s  17,487 ms       105  313 s (54.7 %)
     500     0         1    475 s   2,766 ms        50  156 s (32.8 %)
     500   500         1    616 s   2,703 ms        61  112 s (18.2 %)
     100   100         1    647 s   2,020 ms        48  154 s (23.8 %)
     100   400         1  1,330 s   8,859 ms        98  178 s (13.4 %)
     500     0         4    382 s   1,938 ms        44   26 s (6.7 %)

"rows" is the rows per commit, "pause" the wait after each commit in
milliseconds, "appliers" wsrep_slave_threads, "run" the length of the
clean-up, "longest" the slowest insert during it, "over 1 s" the
inserts that took longer than 1 s, and "paused" the time the cluster
spent paused by flow control. Before each run the same inserts take
7 ms at the median. 500 rows per commit cut the longest wait from
17.5 s to 2.8 s, below the 10 s socket bound, and halve the time under
flow control. Neither a smaller batch nor a pause improves on that:
with one applier thread the cluster spends 112 to 178 s under flow
control whatever the pacing, and the run gets up to 2.8 times longer.
More applier threads are a change to the database
(C5C3/cobaltcore#1835).

The patch sets `DEFAULT_CLEANUP_BATCH_SIZE` to 500, the number of ids
one statement names already (`_IDS_PER_STATEMENT`), so a default batch
is one statement. It adds `DEFAULT_CLEANUP_BATCH_PAUSE_MS = 0` and a
module-level `_batch_pause` in seconds: `_run_batches` sleeps that
long after each committed batch and never after the empty query that
ends a loop, so a batch that wrap_db_retry runs several times pauses
once, after its commit. The pause is off by default, as the rig showed
no benefit; it lets a deployment try one without a new image.
`clean_command` takes `batch_pause_ms`, sets `_batch_pause` for the
length of its run and resets it to 0 first thing in its `finally`, so
the helpers called on their own, as the upstream tests call them,
never pause. A keyword threaded through the ten helpers between
`clean_command` and `_run_batches` would instead touch every signature
the upstream batching commit a28ddb94c3 introduced.

A run of 500-row batches issues 20 times as many id queries as one of
10,000. No index covers their filters (`deleted`, `deleted_at`,
`expiration`), so each id query walked the primary key from the first
row and read again every row the batches before it had passed: for a
table with L rows that stay and N rows to change, about
L * N / (2 * batch size) rows per pass. `_run_batch` therefore orders
the ids by the id column, the first column of the id query, reads only
the ids above `after`, the last id of the batch before, and returns the
last id it read with the count; `_run_batches` passes it on, and a batch
that wrap_db_retry runs again keeps its `after`. The order changes no
write set, so the Galera measurement above still holds. Measured on
2026-11-20 on one mariadb:12.3 (12.3.3) container with a 2 GB buffer
pool, 250,000 secrets with five store and two user metadata rows each,
and an ACL of two users on 70,000 of them. One run with
`--soft-delete-expired-secrets` changes 460,000 rows: it removes 30,000
soft-deleted secrets with their metadata and the ACLs of 20,000 expired
secrets, and soft-deletes those with their metadata:

  rows    id order      run  rows scanned
  10,000        no   56.3 s        24.3 M
     500        no  241.0 s       367.5 M
     500       yes   37.6 s         4.2 M
  10,000       yes   45.9 s         4.2 M

"rows scanned" is the sum of Handler_read_rnd_next and Handler_read_next
over the run. Three more runs of the final code in 500-row batches, two
of them with the built image, took 60.0 to 71.2 s and scanned the same
4.2 M rows: the run time on this rig varies with the load of the host,
the rows scanned do not. The id query of the ACL users joins three
tables. MariaDB reads that join from `secret_acls` and sorts all of it
for every batch (EXPLAIN: `Using temporary; Using filesort`), so `after`
bounds none of its reads; a semi-join, `acl_id IN (SELECT ...)`, gets
the same plan. The patch therefore starts that query with `SELECT
STRAIGHT_JOIN` for the `mysql` and `mariadb` dialects. MariaDB then
reads `secret_acl_users` by a range of its primary key from `after` and
stops at the batch size; the other dialects get the query unchanged.
After the 20,000 expired secrets are soft-deleted, the pass that deletes
their 40,000 ACL users took 11.4 s and scanned 12.3 M rows in 500-row
batches without the prefix, 2.0 s and 0.14 M rows with it, and 1.6 s and
0.86 M rows in 10,000-row batches without the id order. The "yes" rows
above include the prefix; without it the 500-row run took 42.9 s and
scanned 17.1 M rows. For every other id query of the clean-up, EXPLAIN
on the rig shows a range of the primary key and no filesort.

`clean_command` raises `ValueError` for a `batch_size` below 1 and a
negative `batch_pause_ms` before it touches the configuration or the
database, and logs both values once at INFO: "Clean-up batches: 500
rows per commit, 0 ms pause after each commit". `barbican-manage db
clean` gains `--batch-size`/`-b` (default 500) and `--batch-pause-ms`
(default 0) and passes both to `clean_command`; its `main()` turns the
`ValueError` into `ERROR: batch_size must be at least 1, got 0` and
exit code 1. The legacy `barbican-db-manage clean` passes its
`--batch-size`, whose default is the same constant, and gets no pause
option. The barbican-operator passes the two flags only when
`spec.dbClean.batchSize` or `.batchPauseMilliseconds` is set, because
an image without this patch answers them with `unrecognized
arguments` and exit code 2.

Tests: `test_db_clean_no_args` and `test_db_clean_with_args`
(barbican/tests/cmd/test_barbican_manage.py) pin the exact keyword
arguments of `clean_command` and move with the patch: they expect
`batch_size` and `batch_pause_ms` with their defaults. The patch adds
`test_db_clean_with_batch_args`, which passes `--batch-size 200
--batch-pause-ms 50`. No other upstream test depends on the
10,000-row default. barbican/tests/cmd/test_db_cleanup.py gains nine
tests of `clean_command`: three pauses of 7 ms and four commits for
five rows in batches of 2, no pause with 0 ms, no pause when nothing
is cleaned, the defaults of 500 rows and no pause with their INFO
line, no pause from a helper called on its own before and after a
paused run, one pause for a batch retried after `DBDeadlock`, no pause
and a reset value after the last retry, and the two `ValueError`s
raised before the database is set up. They patch `time.sleep` and
count only the non-zero waits, because oslo.db calls `time.sleep(0)`
on each sqlite connection checkout. A tenth test runs `_run_batches`
in batches of 2 over five rows its apply function leaves in place and
expects each id read once, in order. An eleventh compiles the id query
of the ACL users for `mysql+pymysql://`, `mariadb+pymysql://` and
`sqlite://` and expects `SELECT STRAIGHT_JOIN` for the first two only.
The retry patch's
`test_run_batch_names_a_bounded_number_of_ids_per_statement` moves: it
takes the last id from `_run_batch` with the count. The eleven tests and
the three barbican-manage tests fail without the change to the code.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_barbican.sh (Test 13) asserts in
the built image the two defaults, both options with their defaults in
`barbican-manage db clean --help`, three pauses of 7 ms for five rows
in batches of 2, and the error line and exit code 1 of `--batch-size
0`.

The twins under patches/barbican/2026.1/ and patches/barbican/2026.2/
carry the same diff and differ in their Applies-to line only.

Applies to barbican 22.0.0 (2026.1), on top of 0001 and 0002.

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

